Byzantine-Islamic Frontier Database — Licensing
===============================================

This repository contains multiple components released under different licenses.
This file is an orientation, not a license text in itself. The full text of each
license is in the referenced file, and the reasoning behind the split is set out
in the Governance Charter, section 8 (docs/governance.md, "Licensing").

Components and their licenses
-----------------------------

1. DATA — Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)
   Full text: LICENSE-DATA
   Applies to: the database records (records/), dating-system reference data
   (data/), and the authored *content* of the controlled vocabularies
   (the labeled concepts in vocabularies/).
   Attribution is fulfilled by citing the project and the canonical identifier
   of the relevant record; see the citation guidance in README.md.

2. CODE — Apache License 2.0
   Full text: LICENSE-CODE
   Applies to: the tooling and validators (tools/), all *.py files, and any
   executable scripts in the repository.

3. VOCABULARY STRUCTURE and SCHEMA — Creative Commons Zero 1.0 Universal (CC0 1.0)
   Full text: LICENSE-VOCAB
   Applies to: the JSON Schema (schema/byzfrontier_schema_v1.json) and the
   skeletal structure of the SKOS vocabularies — their taxonomic organization
   and concept scheme, as distinct from the labeled concept content authored by
   the project (which is DATA, above).

On the vocabulary content / vocabulary structure distinction
------------------------------------------------------------

The split within vocabularies/ is deliberate. The schema and the skeletal
ontology (how concepts are organized) are released under CC0 so they can be
reused, embedded, and built upon with no restriction, maximising
interoperability. The specific labeled concept content the project has authored
(definitions, scope notes, and the scholarly choices they embody) is authored
scholarship and is released under CC BY 4.0, so that reuse carries attribution.
Where the boundary is genuinely ambiguous, treat the material as DATA (CC BY 4.0).

Source quotations
-----------------

Direct quotations from primary sources in public-domain editions are unrestricted.
Quotations from modern critical editions and translations are used for evidential
purposes under fair-use / fair-dealing provisions, with the edition and translator
recorded in the attestation record. See docs/governance.md, section 8.3.

SPDX summary
------------

  records/, data/, vocabularies/ (content)      CC-BY-4.0
  tools/, *.py, scripts                          Apache-2.0
  schema/, vocabularies/ (structure)             CC0-1.0
